Subject: drm/nouveau: declare the channel dead when fences stop retiring

On nv4x (tested: G73 / GeForce 7600 GS) the graphics engine faults under
sustained GL load and never retires another fence. Measured with a probe in
nouveau_fence_wait_legacy: 790 hits on the fence deadline, done=0 every single
time, 760 of them on Xorg's channel. The waits reaching that point are long --
698 at nouveau_gem_ioctl_cpu_prep's 30s and 78 at MAX_SCHEDULE_TIMEOUT from
nouveau_bo_move -- so userspace waits, times out, retries, forever.

The desktop is livelocked rather than deadlocked, which is why X keeps
answering non-GL clients while every GL request hangs and the machine appears
to need a power cycle.

nouveau already knows how to recover from a dead channel:
nouveau_channel_killed() sets chan->killed and calls
nouveau_fence_context_kill(), after which nouveau_gem_ioctl_pushbuf() returns
-ENODEV and the client exits cleanly. That path is driven by a FIFO
notification which never arrives on this chip, so nothing ever declares the
channel dead.

Trigger the same recovery from the fence's own deadline. fence->timeout is
absolute -- set to jiffies + 15s at emit -- so it survives across the repeated
short waits, unlike anything measured from function entry.

Result on the reference machine: previously wedged within 1-3 soaks under every
Mesa from 21.3.9 to 26.1.8 and on kernels 6.1 and 6.18. With this patch the
engine still dies (that defect is untouched) but the desktop survives: Xorg
stays out of D state, X keeps serving, and the GL client takes the error.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
@@ nouveau_fence_wait_legacy()
 		t = jiffies;
 
+		/*
+		 * The engine is dead -- declare the channel dead too, so
+		 * userspace gets -ENODEV instead of retrying forever.
+		 *
+		 * Everything touching chan stays inside the RCU section: an
+		 * earlier revision used chan->fence after rcu_read_unlock().
+		 */
+		if (time_after_eq(t, fence->timeout)) {
+			struct nouveau_channel *chan;
+			int chid = -1;
+
+			rcu_read_lock();
+			chan = rcu_dereference(fence->channel);
+			/*
+			 * ⛔ DO NOT call nouveau_fence_context_kill() here.
+			 * nouveau_channel_killed() does, but it runs from an
+			 * nvif notify callback -- a different context. Called
+			 * from inside the wait it re-enters fctx->lock and
+			 * walks the pending list mid-mutation: NULL deref at
+			 * 0x4 after roughly 60 firings. Setting chan->killed
+			 * is enough; pushbuf already checks it.
+			 */
+			if (chan && !atomic_xchg(&chan->killed, 1))
+				chid = chan->chid;
+			rcu_read_unlock();
+
+			if (chid >= 0)
+				pr_err("nouveau: channel %d stopped retiring fences - marking it dead\n",
+				       chid);
+			__set_current_state(TASK_RUNNING);
+			return -ENODEV;
+		}
+
 		if (wait != MAX_SCHEDULE_TIMEOUT && time_after_eq(t, timeout)) {
 			__set_current_state(TASK_RUNNING);
 			return 0;
 		}
